---

<Base
  path={footerPage('/privacy').href}
  title={`Privacy - ${siteName}`}
  description="What happens to your files, passwords and anything you type or draw when you use a tool on this site."
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p><strong>{draftSentence}</strong> It was written from what the site’s code does. The person who runs this site has not yet checked it.</p>

  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every tool on this site does its work inside your browser, on your device. Your files are not uploaded, and neither is any password, text, drawing or picture you give a tool.</li>
    <li>There are no accounts. The site sets no cookies and writes nothing to your browser’s storage.</li>
    <li>There are no adverts, and no analytics or tracking scripts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What happens to your file</h2>
  <p>You choose a file with the button, by dropping it on the page or by pasting it. Your browser reads it from your device. No tool on this site runs on a server today.</p>
  <p>
    The work is done by parts of the page that run in the background, called workers. The page hands your file to the tool’s worker inside your browser, and that worker makes the new file there. To
    read a PDF and draw its pages, the tool’s worker starts a second worker and hands it the file too. Both workers get the file’s password, if it has one. The pictures of your pages are drawn by the
    workers too. You save the new file with the Download button. Nothing is saved on its own.
  </p>
  <p>After you choose a file, the page fetches the programs that read and write PDFs. They come from this site. These requests carry nothing from your file: not its contents, not its name and not a password.</p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-uniform-requests): tied to its claim table and to tests/e2e/uniform-requests.spec.ts. */}
  <p>Which programs the page fetches does not depend on what your file holds. This is true for a damaged file, a protected file or a file of the wrong kind too.</p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-compress-to-target, design decision 11): a file at or under the page's size is held, so no job set is fetched for it. */}
  <p>
    On the Compress PDF to 100 KB, 200 KB, 500 KB, 1 MB and 2 MB pages, a file already at or under the page’s size starts no job, so the programs a job needs are not fetched for it.
  </p>
  <p>Your file’s name is shown on the page and used to name the new file.</p>
  <p>
    A new PDF can carry the old file’s title, author and other details, its accessibility tags and its attached files. The tags can bring along what was typed in form fields on pages you left out. In
    Merge PDF, the title, author, other details and attached files come from the first file.
  </p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-crop-pdf): tied to its design, section 9, and to tests/unit/crop-pdf.test.ts. */}
  <p>
    Crop PDF does not remove what it cuts off. It stays in the new file, where anyone who has the file can get it back, and some programs can still find, copy or read aloud the text in it.
  </p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-grayscale-pdf): tied to its design, section 10, and to tests/unit/grayscale-pdf.test.ts (pictures' data and colour values unchanged). */}
  <p>Grayscale PDF shows colours in grey but keeps the colours of the pages’ drawing and pictures in the new file, where a program can still read them.</p>
  <p>
    Pictures from PDF to JPG and PDF to PNG hold only the drawn page, with what was typed in its form fields as the page shows it, and no title, author or date. Image to PDF copies no details of your
    pictures, such as the camera, date or place, into the PDF.
  </p>

  <h2>How long the page holds your file</h2>
  <p>
    The page holds your file in your browser, on your device, while you work on it. It lets go of the file when you remove or replace it, when you press “Start again”, and when you close the tab or
    open another page in the same tab. Switching to another tab keeps it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In tools that show every page of one file, the workers keep the file open, with its password if it has one, to draw the pictures of its pages. They close it when you remove or replace the file,
    before each job, and when the workers are closed. In the other tools, the workers keep nothing open between one picture and the next.
  </p>
  <p>The new file is held in the same way until you start another job, press “Start again” or leave the page.</p>
  <p>
    The tool’s worker is closed after every job, and the second worker is closed with it, so they keep nothing from one job to the next. They are also closed when you press Cancel, when you press
    “Start again” and when you leave the page.
  </p>

  <h2>Passwords</h2>
  <h3>The password of a protected file</h3>
  <p>A tool asks for this password when you choose a PDF that needs one. The password goes to the workers, inside your browser, to open the file.</p>
  <p>The page keeps the password while the file is on the page, so you do not have to type it again after Cancel or a failed job. A wrong try is kept until you type another.</p>
  <p>
    The page forgets the password when the job has made the new file, or told you no new file was needed. It also forgets it when you replace or remove the file, when you press “Start again”, and when
    you leave the page.
  </p>
  <p>The Remove a password tool takes off the password you type. It also takes off print and edit restrictions, which needs no password. The new copy has no password, so anyone who has it can open it.</p>

  <h3>A new password, in Protect with a password</h3>
  <p>You type the new password twice. Only what you type in the first box goes to the tool’s worker, with the job. The second box stays on the page, to check that both match.</p>
  <p>
    The page keeps the new password through Cancel and a failed job, and when you choose another file in its place. It forgets the new password when the job has made the protected file. It also
    forgets it when you press “Start again”, when you remove the file, and when you leave the page.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site does not have your password, so it cannot get it back for you. A password does not hide everything in a file. The Protect with a password page lists what can still be seen without it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your browser may offer to save a password you type, or to suggest one. A saved password is kept by your browser and may be copied to your browser account. That is your browser’s offer, and you can
    turn it down.
  </p>

  <h2>Text, drawings and pictures</h2>
  <p>
    Watermark asks for the text to draw. Sign PDF asks for your name, a drawing of your signature or a picture of it. What you give goes to the tool’s worker with the job, as your file does, and is drawn into
    the new file. In Sign PDF, only the way you chose goes with the job.
  </p>
  <p>A drawing goes as a list of points. The page does not turn it into a picture. A picture you choose is read by the tool’s worker, like your file.</p>
  <p>
    The page keeps what you gave while you work, so you can try again after Cancel or a failed job. That includes what you gave under another way in Sign PDF. The page lets go of all of it when you
    press “Start again”, when you remove the file, and when you leave the page.
  </p>

  <h2>Scripts and the home page</h2>
  <p>Every script on the site comes from the site itself. What you type in the box on the home page to find a tool stays on the page.</p>

  <h2>What your browser may do</h2>
  <p>This site cannot change these.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your browser keeps a list of your downloads, with each file’s name.</li>
    <li>It may keep copies of this site’s pages and programs in its cache, as it does for any site. Your files are not among them.</li>
    <li>Browser extensions you have added can read what is on a page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the page may load and send</h2>
  <p>
    Each page gives your browser a rule, called a Content Security Policy. Under it, your browser blocks the page from loading scripts and other files from any other address, and from sending requests
    to any other address.
  </p>
  <p>In Chrome and Firefox, the site’s tests check that the same rule also holds in workers started the same way as those that read your file. It has not yet been checked in Safari.</p>
  <p>
    The rule does not stop following a link, opening another page, a direct connection of the kind used for video calls (WebRTC), or looking up another site’s address in advance (DNS prefetch). It
    does not limit requests to this site’s own address, which on GitHub Pages is shared by every site of the same GitHub account. The site’s tests check that no request carries your file, its name or a
    password.
  </p>

  <h2>Who serves the site</h2>
  <p>
    This site is served by GitHub Pages, a service run by GitHub. Each time your browser asks for a page or one of the site’s programs, GitHub receives your IP address, the address of each page it asks
    for and the details your browser sends with the request, and may record them. Your files are not sent to GitHub.
  </p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-uniform-requests). The last two sentences are its open question 1; the cases they cover are named in its design. */}
  <p>
    From your browser’s requests, GitHub can tell which tool page you opened and when you chose a file: in practice your first file, and later ones once your browser’s copies of
    the programs are about 10 minutes old, or if your browser keeps no copies. It may be able to tell when you started a job. GitHub does not receive your file, its name or a password. Your
    browser may ask for a program more than once, and in a few cases how often it asks can depend on your file. If a page of your file is slow to show, asking again can tell GitHub that.
  </p>
  {/* Draft words for the owner (add-compress-to-target, design decision 11): whether a job's programs are fetched on those pages depends on the file's size. */}
  <p>
    On the Compress PDF to 100 KB, 200 KB, 500 KB, 1 MB and 2 MB pages, when the programs a job needs are fetched, GitHub can tell that your file was larger than that page’s size. It cannot tell the
    file’s contents, its name or its exact size.
  </p>
</Base>
